  about: 'These nine short verses are where it all begins — the first hymn of the first book of the Rig Veda, and therefore the opening words of the oldest continuously recited literature on earth. For three thousand years, anyone approaching the Veda has met Agni, fire, first. And that is itself a choice worth pausing on. The tradition could have opened with a hymn to its most powerful god, or its most beautiful, or the mystery of creation. Instead it opens with fire — the practical, domestic, tended thing that cooks the food and warms the house and carries the offering upward; the one god you can actually light with your own hands. Agni is the mediator, the one who stands between earth and heaven and carries messages both ways. To begin the whole vast collection with him is to say: the sacred is not far off. It starts at the hearth, with a flame you kindle yourself, and it ends — as the very last hymn will show — with people asking to live together in peace. Between this fire and that concord lies everything the Veda has to say.',
